<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cis-sutlej-states">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cis-Sutlej states</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cis-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a> states were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> and other states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> between the Sutlej and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> rivers, among them Patiala, Nabha and Jind. To avoid absorption by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, they accepted British protection in 1809, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amritsar-1809">Treaty of Amritsar</a> with Ranjit Singh that year fixed the Sutlej as his border. Many smaller states were later annexed, while the largest survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> until 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cis-sutlej-states.jpg" alt="Cis-Sutlej states" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reduced and drawn by S. Abdos Sobhan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_and_native_states_in_the_Cis-Sutlej_Division_between_1847%E2%80%9351,_by_Abdos_Sobhan,_1858_(F.4-27).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
